{"title":"Origin","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>origin</b> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Coordinate\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Coordinate\">coordinate</a> system is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Center\" title=\"Center\">center</a> point or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Zero\" title=\"Zero\">zero</a> point where the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Axe&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Axe (page does not exist)\">axes</a> meet.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"In_Euclidean_Systems\">In Euclidean Systems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Origin&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: In Euclidean Systems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the Euclidean <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Plane\" title=\"Plane\">plane</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/a/00a62d72646c467ae02878bc6dd7138e087a13c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^2$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />, the origin is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />. Similarly, in the Euclidean <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Space\" title=\"Space\">space</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba438cedaa8f58c80eb239e792bd4cdeba1910ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^3$\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" />, the origin is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/9/4e9f36b13a11a14accae8e072c0cf34d69267bb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"59\" height=\"20\" />. This way, in general, the origin of an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-dimensional Euclidean space <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/9/359c93f77ee9ad4fdfaae8b7c46a2d7ed91a3b29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}^n$\" width=\"21\" height=\"12\" /> is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-tuple <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/9/d39b8dec7eaefbad52cf6aa05601811b41407d7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,\\ldots,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" /> with all its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> components equal to zero.\n</p><p>Thus, the origin of any coordinate system is the point where all of its components are equal to zero.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Origin&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018003036\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.010 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.015 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 40/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 259/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1511/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.438      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.438      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5702:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018003036 and revision id 228534. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}